The Plenum Paradigm: A New Architecture for Resilient AI and Social Systems

1. Introduction: Beyond Brittle Systems

Our current digital civilization is built upon a fragmented foundation. The artificial intelligence and social platforms that mediate our lives are engineered from a patchwork of competing principles, leading to systemic instability, polarization, and, in some cases, outright collapse. These are not isolated bugs but predictable outcomes of a design philosophy that prioritizes short-term optimization over long-term coherence. This design philosophy is not an academic concern; it is the direct cause of catastrophic brand failures, collapsing civic forums, and AI systems that cannibalize their own training data, representing billions in wasted investment and immeasurable social harm. To build a more resilient future, we require a new architectural paradigm—one grounded in a unified understanding of how structure, agency, and meaning emerge and persist.

This analysis presents the Relativistic Scalar-Vector Plenum (RSVP) framework as this new foundation. RSVP offers a first-principles approach, providing a single, coherent language to model the dynamics of stability across cosmological, cognitive, and social scales. By synthesizing the core principles of RSVP, we propose a new paradigm for technology leaders and policymakers, shifting the focus from brittle, high-performance systems to thermodynamically stable architectures designed for longevity and resilience.

Our central argument is that a fundamental shift in design philosophy is both necessary and achievable. We must move from the extractive, optimization-focused models that currently dominate toward a plenum-based architecture that prioritizes non-extractive entropy economics and semantic governance. This transition is not merely a technical upgrade; it is an essential step toward building a sustainable and meaningful digital future.


