2. The Foundational Crisis: Extractive Dynamics and Universal Collapse

Understanding the failure modes of our digital systems is a matter of strategic importance. The recurrent crises we observe—from AI model collapse to the decay of online civic discourse—are not disconnected events. They are the predictable consequences of a flawed design philosophy built on fragmented ontologies, where the principles governing data, algorithms, and user interaction are treated as distinct and unrelated domains. This partitioning obstructs our view of the shared dynamical principles that govern the rise and fall of all complex systems.

The dominant model today is one of semantic extraction. Platforms are designed to extract value (attention, data, engagement) from their internal environment, a process that inevitably increases systemic disorder, or entropy. This leads to a state of entropy saturation, where the system becomes so cluttered with noise, contradiction, and low-quality information that its structural integrity fails. Coherent communication breaks down, trust erodes, and the platform's core function collapses.

The RSVP framework reveals that these failures are not unique to digital platforms but are universal signatures of systemic decay. The same patterns of collapse appear across all scales of structured existence, from cognitive processes to cosmological structures.

